
	    Overview of Lab Data Systems' Floppy Disk Handlers


     Lab Data Systems offers several high-performance floppy disk handlers
for the OS/8 (OS/78) operating system.  These handlers provide several dif-
ferent formats, optimized for different systems, as explained below.  With
two exceptions, both System and Non-system versions are available.  The sys-
tem handlers all boot up with either the RX01 or RX02 bootstrap and can be
used in either the left- or right-hand drive on either type of system.  It
should perhaps be emphasized, however, that handlers using 'byte-mode' for-
mats can only be used with Single Density diskettes due to hardware limita-
tions.  Single Density disks can be used with any DEC-compatible floppy disk
system that emulates the RX01 or RX02 (i.e. DSD-210/440, possibly others).

     The system handlers preserve the date during a bootstrap operation and
support both Drive 0 and Drive 1 as 'co-resident' devices.  The latter fea-
ture means that programs do not need to load a special handler for Drive 1,
as they must when using the conventional system handler.  This in turn means
that data on Drive 1 can be accessed more rapidly, and programs which trade
buffer size for handler space (PAL8 for example) can now use larger buffers.
Another benefit of having co-resident handlers is that 'virtual disk' files
(implemented by Lab Data Systems DSKN handler) can reside on either device.
It should be noted, though, that when a system disk is booted up on Drive 1
only that drive can be accessed, since all references to Drive 0 are mapped
to Drive 1.  (Such operation is normally restricted to times when Drive 0 is
not functional.)

     The non-system handlers all have three entry points: drive 0, drive 1,
and drive 2 (for DSD-210 systems only).  The non-system handlers will work
on either RX01 or RX02 systems, but again -only- with single-density disks.
For convenience in reading different kinds of disks, a version of the non-
system handler is available that supports 3 different formats, rather than
3 different drives.  (In that version the 'drive number' may be changed by
a 'SET' command - it is normally Drive 1).  The non-system handlers permit
the number of 'retry' operations to be changed, which increases the chance
of recovering data from a damaged diskette (the default is one retry).

     Storage capacity: the major advantage of a byte-mode handler is the
improved data storage available; thus the 'raw' storage capacity of a sin-
gle density disk is 667 blocks in byte-mode vs. 494 blocks for the conven-
tional handler.  For system disks (on which some of the space is reserved)
the difference is approximately 40% (611 free blocks on a byte-mode disk,
vs. 438 on conventional disks).  Note that the additional 173 blocks are
obtained simply by changing the handler - no changes to the formatting of
the disk are involved (unlike the use of double-density disks).  Users who
absolutely need more space however, can purchase specially-formatted disks
from Computer Methods Co., which, when used with the 'H' handler described
below, allow one to get up to 770 blocks on a single-density disk.

     Double-density (RX02) systems, of course, get 2*494, or 988 blocks per
diskette.  As mentioned above, a double-density byte-mode handler cannot be
written due to hardware limitations.  This is too bad, since such a handler
would get 1334 blocks on one disk!  As an alternative, one can use the RTPIP
program (available from Lab Data Systems), to store OS/8 files on a double-
density RT-11 disk.  This is less convenient because the files can not be
accessed by general OS/8 programs, but it does increase the storage capacity
of a diskette by 25%.
     System requirements: the non-system handlers use two pages of memory
and will operate on even a minimal 8K system, although some Fortran-II pro-
grams may require re-compilation (and possibly other changes) if they were
written for use with only 1-page handlers.  Not all languages support the
use of two-page handlers in an 8K environment.  To use the System handlers
one must have at least 12K, as well as the proper version of programs such
as Fortran-IV or Basic, since these languages must make special provisions
for '12K' system handlers.  All system software with release dates of 1979
or later should work without any problems, with the exception of the print
spooler symbiont which runs on a DECstation.  A patch is available for this
program, however, which allows it to be used with either byte-mode or con-
ventional system disks.  The program PIP.SV must also be patched before a
byte-mode disk can be properly '.ZEROed'.

     The formats available are as follows:

1.   'D' format: this format is compatible with that used by the RT-11 oper-
ating system.  Thus data recorded in this format can be read on a PDP11 sys-
tem, using one of the 'block mode' options in the DUMP command.  In addition
to the ability to exchange data between systems, this handler offers another
advantage: it has the highest throughput of any single-density format using
standard disks (over 23 blocks/second, vs. 13 blocks/second for the conven-
tional handler).  The 'D' handler is also available in a RTS/8 version.

2.   'F' format: this is a variation of 'D' format which is optimized for
use with '6100' microprocessor systems.  The data rate of the standard 'D'
format is too high for 6100 systems to keep up with, with the result that
such systems can read only one sector per disk rotation (equivalent to a
throughput of about 2 blocks/second).  'F' format reduces the data rate by
50%, which allows 6100 systems to operate at full speed (12 blocks/second -
essentially the same speed as with the conventional handler), while still
gaining 40% more data storage.  Due to the close relationship between 'D'
and 'F' formats, the non-system handler can be used with either one simply
by changing a 'switch' in the handler.  If this switch is set to '0' the
handler uses 'D' format, while if it set to '1', it uses 'F' format.

3.   'R' format: this is the format used by Steuart Dewar's byte-mode hand-
ler.  This format cannot be used for a system disk, but because a RX01 ver-
sion of this handler is in the public domain, this format is sometimes used
for data exchange.  Note that Lab Data's version of this handler will work
on either RX01 or RX02 systems.  The throughput is approximately 17 blocks
per second - intermediate between 'D' and 'F' formats.

4.   'C' format: this format is compatible with the COS-8 operating system.
The immediate attraction of this format is that the directory structure of a
COS-8 disk is similar to that of an OS/8 disk (except for the date offset) -
hence by using this handler OS/8 programs can access COS-8 files.  This is
not quite ase useful as it sounds however, because (a) COS-8 programs ('.SV'
files) will not run under OS/8, (b) COS-8 data files do not use ASCII, hence
special conversion programs are necessary to move data between the two sys-
tems, and (c) the transfer rate on 6100 systems is only 1.7 blocks/second -
the slowest of all formats, due in part to the fact that the data is stored
in a VERY STRANGE way!  Because of the limited utility of this format, plus
the fact that the bootstrap does not conform to OS/8 requirements, Lab Data
Systems does not offer a 'C-format' system handler.

5.   'H' format: this handler uses the specially formatted 'huge' floppies
supplied by Computer Methods Co.  These disks store a total of 1540 blocks
using both sides of a 'flippy' disk (only one side can be accessed at a
time), and have a transfer rate of 17.3 blocks/second.  Users with systems
based on the '6100' processor (VT/78, PCM12) must use disks formatted for
those machines - otherwise the transfer rate drops to 2 blocks/second (as
it does for everything but the special 'F' format).

6.   CP/M format: this handler allows programs such as COPCOM to move data
between OS/8 and CP/M systems.  Since the directory structures of the two
systems are quite different, access to data is limited to block-by-block
transfers.  (A 'CPMPIP' program similar to RTPIP will be offered if there
is sufficient interest.)  This handler can also be used with other formats
simply by changing the 'interleave factor' with a '.SET' command.

7.   For use on RX02 systems, Lab Data Systems also offers a 'universal' 12-
bit system handler which supports both Drive 0 and Drive 1 as 'co-resident'
devices, preserves the system date during a bootstrap, and works with either
single- or double-density diskettes.  This handler is completely compatible
with the conventional single- and double-density handlers, but by providing
support for both drives it frees up a scarce 'handler slot' for other uses.
Either single- or double-density may be used with this handler.  Single-
density disks can be booted up on either RX01 or RX02 systems that have at
least 12K of memory.  The throughput for single-density disks is 13 blocks
per second; for double-density disks it is 26 blocks/second on an 8/e, but
only 19 blocks/second on 6100 systems.

     Another 'universal' handler of sorts is the '3-format' version of the
non-system byte-mode handler.  This handler has entry points for 'D', 'F',
and 'R' formatted disks, and thus allows data on such disks to be easily
moved from one to another with the convenience of having only one handler
to do it.  Users who have both RX01 and RX02 systems at their disposal of-
ten find that the combination of the 'universal' 12-bit system handler and
the 'universal' 8-bit non-system handler allows them to make optimal use of
both disk systems.

     Nomenclature:  The device names 'D0','D1','D2' stand for 'Disk 0/1/2'.
The names 'F0-F2' stand for 'Floppy 0-2', while 'H0-H2' are used for 'huge'
floppies. 'C0-C2' are used for the COS-8 compatible format, while 'R0-R2'
are Dewar's shorthand for the conventional names 'RXA0', 'RXA1', etc.  The
CP/M compatible handler uses the names 'A','B','C'.  For reference purposes
here are the filenames and 'BUILD' names of the handlers described above:

     RX11SY     RXSY	'D' format system handler (RT11 data format)
     RX11NS     RX11	'D' non-system handler (switchable to 'F' format)

     RX61SY     RXSY	'F' format system handler (for 6100 systems)
     RX61NS     RX61	'F' non-system handler (switchable to 'D' format)

     RXDEWR     DEWR	'R' format non-system handler (Dewar compatible)
     RX3FMT     RX3F	'D','F','R' format non-system handler

     RXCOS8     RXC8	'C' format non-system handler (COS-8 compatible)
     RXCPMX     CPMX     Adjustable format for reading 'foreign' disks

     RXHFSY     HFSY	'H' format system handler (for 'huge' floppies)
     RXHFNS     RXHF	'H' format non-system handler

     RX2SYS     RX8E	 Universal 12-bit system handler for RX01/RX02
			 systems, single- or double-density diskettes
     Floppy handlers are available from a number of different sources (e.g.
DECUS, etc.).  In comparing other handlers with the ones described above, the
following questions may provide some useful insight.  Lab Data Systems hand-
lers were (of course!) designed to satisfy these criteria:

     a) Will the handler work on both RX01 and RX02 systems?  Many do not
     because byte-mode is different on the two systems.

     b) Will the handler work on all 12-bit machines?  A surprising num-
     ber of authors use non-standard instructions such as 'BSW' or the
     MQ register in order to save a few words.  Such handlers will not
     work on non-Omnibus machines and thus are not true OS/8 handlers.

     c) Is a system handler available?  Since system handlers are much
     harder to write, frequently only non-system handlers are offered.

     d) If a system handler is available, does it use the standard boot-
     strap (there are actually two different standard bootstraps - one
     for RX01 and one for RX02 systems), or does it need a special boot-
     strap?  If the handler is to work on DECstation products it must
     work interchangeably with either standard bootstrap.

     e) Can the system handler be booted up on either drive?  This is
     not essential until Drive 0 fails!

     f) Does the system handler preserve the system date when it is
     booted up?  This is also not essential, but exceedingly conven-
     ient, since otherwise files are created without any date.

     g) Can the system handler be installed with BUILD or is a special
     installation procedure required?

     h) Does the system handler deal properly with errant calls to 7700?

     i) Is a RTS/8 version available?  This may not be important for
     single-user systems, but vital for multi-tasking applications.

     j) Is a patch available for the OS/78 symbiont?  This affects only
     DECstation users who use the print spooler.

